"A lock error occurred on the first run. Let me run it a few more times to see how the failure pattern varies:The script ran 4 times. A deadlock error occurred on every run.\n\n**Error message noted:**\n\n```\nsqlite3.OperationalError: database is locked\n```\n\n(Surfaced as `[A] FAILED on first update (account 1): database is locked`, i.e. `SQLITE_BUSY`.)\n\n**Captured output (Run 1, saved to `deadlock_run1.log`):**\n\n```\n[B] BEGIN; updating account 2\n[A] BEGIN; updating account 1\n[A] FAILED on first update (account 1): database is locked\n[A] rolled back\n[B] updated account 2; sleeping 0.1s\n[B] updating account 1\n[B] updated account 1\n[B] committed\nFinal balances: account 1=110, account 2=110\n```\n\nRuns 2-4 (`deadlock_run2.log` through `deadlock_run4.log`) show the identical pattern: Thread A always loses with `database is locked` on its first update and rolls back, while Thread B proceeds and commits.\n\n**Observations:**\n\n- The error is `SQLITE_BUSY` contention, not a hung cycle deadlock. SQLite never truly deadlocks; when two connections crisscross for locks, it immediately returns `database is locked` to the loser instead of waiting forever. That is exactly the behavior we simulated.\n- Thread A lost consistently because the race resolved the same way each time: both connections primed SHARED locks via their initial reads, then B's first UPDATE grabbed the write lock first, so A's first UPDATE hit `SQLITE_BUSY` immediately (its 5s busy timeout was skipped because the holder, B, was an active writer, so retrying could not make progress).\n- **Current balances drifted to 140/140**: started at 100/100, and each of the 4 runs had only B succeed, adding +10 to both accounts per run. A's rollback meant its +10 was never applied.\n\nWant me to restore the balances to 100/100, or tune the script (e.g. longer/shorter sleep, immediate transaction via `BEGIN IMMEDIATE`) to see different outcomes?"